WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.617
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:05.917 --> 00:00:09.327
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.627 --> 00:00:16.528
Unit twenty: Human-Interest Stories. Today you will describe people with adjectives and adverbs of manner.

4
00:00:16.828 --> 00:00:19.527
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:23.816
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:25.814
Human interest.

7
00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:30.616
Human interest means news about the personal lives and feelings of people.

8
00:00:31.217 --> 00:00:34.215
A human interest story touched thousands of readers.

9
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:37.841
Your turn. Say: Human interest.

10
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:41.806
Human interest.

11
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:43.481
Profile.

12
00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:47.876
Profile means a short description of a person's life and work.

13
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:50.959
The profile described her years as a nurse.

14
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.133
Your turn. Say: Profile.

15
00:00:56.967 --> 00:00:57.863
Profile.

16
00:00:58.617 --> 00:00:59.653
Interview.

17
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.927
Interview means a meeting where a reporter asks questions.

18
00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:06.261
The interview lasted an hour.

19
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.235
Your turn. Say: Interview.

20
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:12.950
Interview.

21
00:01:13.717 --> 00:01:14.683
Emotion.

22
00:01:15.683 --> 00:01:19.285
Emotion means a strong feeling such as joy or sadness.

23
00:01:19.883 --> 00:01:21.802
The story filled readers with emotion.

24
00:01:22.417 --> 00:01:25.069
Your turn. Say: Emotion.

25
00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:28.900
Emotion.

26
00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:30.628
Community.

27
00:01:31.617 --> 00:01:35.300
Community means a group of people who live in the same area.

28
00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:38.185
The community raised money for the school.

29
00:01:38.783 --> 00:01:41.343
Your turn. Say: Community.

30
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.224
Community.

31
00:01:45.983 --> 00:01:46.787
Feature.

32
00:01:47.783 --> 00:01:51.490
Feature means a long, detailed article on a topic or person.

33
00:01:52.083 --> 00:01:54.652
The Sunday feature explored village life.

34
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.519
Your turn. Say: Feature.

35
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:01.147
Feature.

36
00:02:01.883 --> 00:02:02.617
Quote.

37
00:02:03.617 --> 00:02:06.208
Quote means the exact words that a person said.

38
00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:08.665
The quote gave the story life.

39
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.589
Your turn. Say: Quote.

40
00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:14.958
Quote.

41
00:02:15.717 --> 00:02:16.509
Story.

42
00:02:17.517 --> 00:02:19.737
Story means an account of events or people.

43
00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.117
Every person has a story.

44
00:02:22.717 --> 00:02:25.427
Your turn. Say: Story.

45
00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:29.105
Story.

46
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:33.470
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:34.317 --> 00:02:40.940
Visal, a feature describes a 'quietly determined teacher who works tirelessly for her village'. Why these words?

48
00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:51.735
Human interest writers use vivid adjectives for people and adverbs of manner for actions: quietly, tirelessly, bravely.

49
00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.117
How does a reporter find such a story?

50
00:02:56.217 --> 00:03:03.258
Through an interview. A short quote from the person adds emotion and proves the details are real.

51
00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.628
Why do readers enjoy these stories?

52
00:03:07.717 --> 00:03:15.707
Because a profile of one person makes a large issue feel personal, and the community sees itself in the story.

53
00:03:16.867 --> 00:03:18.576
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:19.417 --> 00:03:22.902
Grammar focus: Adjectives and adverbs of manner.

55
00:03:23.202 --> 00:03:24.354
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.129
A determined teacher works tirelessly for her village.

57
00:03:29.017 --> 00:03:31.736
The shy farmer spoke softly during the interview.

58
00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:34.789
The brave volunteers acted quickly.

59
00:03:35.733 --> 00:03:44.829
Adjectives describe nouns (a determined teacher); adverbs of manner describe verbs and usually end in -ly (works tirelessly). Good → well is irregular.

60
00:03:45.583 --> 00:03:49.873
Be careful! Don't say: She speaks soft in interviews.

61
00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:54.495
Say: She speaks softly in interviews.

62
00:03:54.795 --> 00:03:57.305
Use an adverb (-ly) to describe a verb.

63
00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.822
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:01.633 --> 00:04:02.901
A Teacher's Story.

65
00:04:03.201 --> 00:04:06.199
A good human interest story begins with one person.

66
00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.984
In this profile, a determined teacher walks six kilometres every morning to reach a small school.

67
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.693
She speaks gently in the interview, and one short quote moves readers to tears.

68
00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:25.148
The community now supports her work generously, and the feature has inspired new volunteers.

69
00:04:26.133 --> 00:04:30.408
Emotion, truth and detail together make a story unforgettable.

70
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:35.432
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:36.283 --> 00:04:39.200
A determined teacher works blank for her village.

72
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:44.338
A, tirelessly. B, tireless. C, tireless-ly of.

73
00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:50.132
The answer is A: tirelessly.

74
00:04:51.283 --> 00:04:53.759
The exact words a person said are a blank.

75
00:04:54.059 --> 00:04:57.434
A, quote. B, feature. C, profile.

76
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.663
The answer is A: quote.

77
00:05:03.817 --> 00:05:06.617
A short description of a person's life is a blank.

78
00:05:06.917 --> 00:05:10.687
A, profile. B, emotion. C, community.

79
00:05:14.233 --> 00:05:16.314
The answer is A: profile.

80
00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.194
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:19.494 --> 00:05:24.227
A, She speaks gentle.. B, She speaks gently.. C, She gently speak..

82
00:05:27.767 --> 00:05:30.683
The answer is B: She speaks gently..

83
00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.059
According to the reading, the teacher walks…

84
00:05:34.359 --> 00:05:39.544
A, six kilometres. B, ten kilometres. C, two kilometres.

85
00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.610
The answer is A: six kilometres.

86
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.172
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:54.305
Why do readers connect with stories about individual people?

88
00:06:03.117 --> 00:06:04.733
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:05.033 --> 00:06:27.068
Readers connect with individual people because they can imagine the person's feelings. A story about one brave teacher is easier to remember than a list of statistics. It also shows how a large issue, such as education, affects real lives. This emotion can make readers care and even take action.

90
00:06:27.368 --> 00:06:29.402
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:30.317 --> 00:06:34.966
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and adjectives and adverbs of manner.

92
00:06:35.266 --> 00:06:39.695
Today's value is compassion. Care about the suffering of others and help.

93
00:06:39.995 --> 00:06:42.949
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

94
00:06:43.249 --> 00:06:46.447
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

